WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.711
This is a mortgage, from the first enquiry to the first payment, rebuilt on Solid.

2
00:00:05.711 --> 00:00:07.738
Twelve organisations take part.

3
00:00:07.738 --> 00:00:11.661
All of them are fictional, and each has its own application.

4
00:00:12.514 --> 00:00:17.365
Today, each of those organisations collects its own copy of the borrower's documents.

5
00:00:17.365 --> 00:00:23.016
Here the borrower, Alex, keeps the data in a pod, and each organisation asks for the part it needs.

6
00:00:23.866 --> 00:00:26.293
The panel on the right is the Solid Lens.

7
00:00:26.293 --> 00:00:30.318
It shows every read, every write and every permission as it happens.

8
00:00:31.172 --> 00:00:34.516
Alex starts with the payroll provider their employer uses.

9
00:00:35.366 --> 00:00:40.825
It signs a statement of employment and income, and writes it to the pod as a verifiable credential.

10
00:00:41.676 --> 00:00:43.661
The bank does the same for savings.

11
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:51.366
And the credit bureau, for a credit summary.

12
00:00:51.366 --> 00:00:56.752
Three issuers, one place, and each record can be checked later without calling the issuer.

13
00:00:57.605 --> 00:00:58.861
Now Alex shops around.

14
00:00:58.861 --> 00:01:06.800
Two lenders ask the same thing: is income at least seven thousand dollars a month, and is the credit score at least six hundred and twenty?

15
00:01:07.649 --> 00:01:09.660
The wallet answers with a proof.

16
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.613
Each lender learns yes or no, and nothing else.

17
00:01:35.946 --> 00:01:40.518
This is everything the second lender holds: an answer, and who vouches for it.

18
00:01:41.368 --> 00:01:45.310
Alex's offer on a house is accepted, so it is time to apply.

19
00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:54.638
The lender asks for access, and the wallet shows what is being asked: which records, for what purpose, until when, and who else will see them.

20
00:01:55.489 --> 00:01:57.097
Approving creates a grant.

21
00:01:57.097 --> 00:01:59.199
You can see it appear in the Lens.

22
00:02:03.755 --> 00:02:06.307
The application fills itself in from the pod.

23
00:02:06.307 --> 00:02:08.405
Every field shows where it came from.

24
00:02:09.255 --> 00:02:15.028
Once the lender has six key items, US rules give it three business days to send a Loan Estimate.

25
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.244
On the lender's side, the loan officer pulls a credit report and runs automated underwriting.

26
00:02:21.244 --> 00:02:25.858
The lender's own credit report does not come from the pod, and the Lens says so.

27
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.579
The underwriting service reads three credentials at source, and checks each signature.

28
00:02:32.429 --> 00:02:37.496
The Loan Estimate arrives in the pod the next day, and Alex confirms they want to go ahead.

29
00:02:40.028 --> 00:02:44.730
The processor orders an appraisal, a title search, a flood check and tax transcripts.

30
00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:58.645
The appraiser is assigned independently of the lender's sales staff.

31
00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:01.477
They read the property record and the purchase contract.

32
00:03:12.018 --> 00:03:15.022
They never see Alex's income, savings or credit.

33
00:03:15.872 --> 00:03:20.391
The title company reads a name and a contract, and writes its commitment to the pod.

34
00:03:23.316 --> 00:03:29.925
The insurer quotes from property facts already in the pod, and writes evidence of insurance once, for the lender to read.

35
00:03:32.166 --> 00:03:36.920
A month on, the lender must check employment again, within ten business days of closing.

36
00:03:37.771 --> 00:03:42.010
That is a fresh credential from the payroll provider, not a phone call.

37
00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:43.622
The loan is clear to close.

38
00:03:44.472 --> 00:03:46.746
The Closing Disclosure lands in the pod.

39
00:03:46.746 --> 00:03:49.305
Three business days must pass before signing.

40
00:03:52.386 --> 00:03:54.619
At the title company, Alex signs.

41
00:03:54.619 --> 00:03:56.513
Signed copies go to the pod.

42
00:04:02.749 --> 00:04:05.124
The deed is recorded with the county.

43
00:04:05.124 --> 00:04:08.848
Solid plays no part here, because land records are public.

44
00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:10.324
The Lens says that too.

45
00:04:11.175 --> 00:04:12.781
The lender sells the loan.

46
00:04:12.781 --> 00:04:17.288
That is between organisations, but the notice to Alex arrives in the pod.

47
00:04:24.974 --> 00:04:27.376
A new company will collect the payments.

48
00:04:27.376 --> 00:04:32.601
It asks for three things: contact details, the insurance policy, and a payment account.

49
00:04:53.718 --> 00:04:56.879
The original lender no longer needs Alex's live data.

50
00:05:02.417 --> 00:05:04.350
So Alex withdraws its access.

51
00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:14.654
When the lender tries to read the bank data again, the pod refuses.

52
00:05:14.654 --> 00:05:18.234
The lender keeps the loan file the law requires it to keep.

53
00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:21.509
What has ended is its access to Alex's data at source.

54
00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:30.093
This is the whole journey on one page: every organisation, every record, every grant, and what each one never saw.

55
00:05:30.943 --> 00:05:32.790
Thirteen applications.

56
00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:33.798
Nine grants.

57
00:05:33.798 --> 00:05:35.645
No documents uploaded.

58
00:05:36.496 --> 00:05:40.524
Everything you saw ran in a browser, on open standards.

59
00:05:40.524 --> 00:05:46.677
Try it yourself from the welcome page, or join the EDMA Solid Community of Practice.

60
00:05:46.677 --> 00:05:48.068
We meet every week.
